A||||25||86SE||2.8 vs 3.4 - ass-o-meter readings......||||5||
Z||000000||86SE||08-21-2000||02:22 PM||||For all of you who made the 2.8 to 3.4 swap.<P>How is it?  Was it worth it, power wise?<P>I don't need to know 0-60 times or anything like that, I just need to know how much more it throws you back into your seat, how hard it pulls at 60-70MPH.<P>I have an auto and will put in a 93-95 Camaro 3.4 in it this fall.  It wont pull as hard as a 5 speed, but it might get close.<P>I guess all I really wanted to know is if the 3.4 was worth the $$ and the work if you did it yourself.<P>How much faster is it?  How quick does 100 come up on the speedo?<P><BR>Thats it!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Thanks<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero<BR>Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention||||reg||5
Z||000001||mrfiero||08-21-2000||02:53 PM||||I drove an '88 GT with an auto. when I worked at the Fiero Factory last a couple of years ago.  I can tell you that the throttle reponse was better and it felt much stronger than a 2.8.  Since it wasn't my car I didn't push for top speed, but it felt very nice. Auto. Fieros seem to lag a bit when the throttle is mashed, but this car seemed to accelerate instantly.<P>All in all I would say it is a worthwhile swap.||||reg||1
Z||000002||86SE||08-21-2000||04:24 PM||||Alright, thats what I like to hear!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero<BR>Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention||||reg||14
Z||000003||Alex4mula||08-21-2000||04:58 PM||||I put a 3.4 myself into my Formula. For easyness and cost you can't beat it especially if you do it yourself. It definetly feels stronger and pulls better in 5th in the highway (5 speed here). Probably in the next month will take it to a dyno for true HP gain results. No one has posted that here for a 3.4.||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfixit58||08-21-2000||05:26 PM||||Alex,<P>Yes, please post your results. I have a 3.4 sitting in my garage that I'm planning on rebuilding for my replacement. I also have a 5-speed. I would also like to know what cam you have. If stock, do you know what year and model car your 3.4 came from. <P>GM offers a performance cam so I'm leaning that way but would still like to know how much Torque/HP the stock cam produces. Also, what other mods do you have? Headers, ported stock manifolds, ignition upgrade,etc?<P>The neat thing would be to dig up a stock 2.8 dyno result, compaired to a stock 3.4, compaired to a 3.4 with performance cam and mild mods. My future plans for the 3.4 call for a turbo. All these combinations would make a great "how-to" performance tech article.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cooter||08-21-2000||07:43 PM||||I have a 94/95 camaro 3.4 in my 88 GT. I used the 4.10 geared 4 speed and let me tell you, this thing rocks! The engine pulls hard up to 6,000 rpm's and still gets a little over 20 MPG in town. I dont street race (much), but I have out accelerated several heavy hitters, including a mid 70's Camaro with a grocery list of go-fast goodies. He could not believe that he got whacked by a "stock" v6  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>The slower they think you are, the more suprised they will be when you smoke them.||||reg||1
Z||000006||86SE||08-22-2000||12:46 AM||||I am yet to port/match and polish my intake and port my exthaust.<P>I'll see if I cant dig up some stock Camaro/Firebird dyno results....<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero<BR>Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention||||reg||1
Z||000007||mshill||08-22-2000||12:59 AM||||I am also considering a 3.4 buildup for my kit car in the future.  I was thinking that if built right (pistons, compression, etc.) a supercharger or turbo could be added later for a real kicker engine.  What compression would you shoot for if you had a turbo or supercharger in mind?||||reg||1
Z||000008||86SE||08-22-2000||01:12 AM||||I would start low, around 8-8.5.<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero<BR>Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention||||reg||1
Z||000009||Alex4mula||08-22-2000||08:27 AM||||I bought my 3.4 from Ed Parks (Fiero Factory) & installed it myself. They are remanufactured Camaro 3.4s with stock Camaro cam. Someone here compared those specs to the GM HO cam and the difference was minimal.<BR>My install has almost all the goodies. Focoa headers, ported TB, plenum & intake, power pulleys, Jacobs wires & ignition, Accel coil and K&N filter. I expect it to be close to 200HP. This week I'll check a local dyno shop $$$. I'm veeeery curious  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||mrfixit58||08-22-2000||08:52 AM||||Alex - I'm looking forward to the results. I've had one local Fiero "know-it-all" claim that a 3.4 upgrade is a waste of time and money. I think he's just likes hearing himself make noise. But, I've got nothing to back up my instincts about the 3.4 conversion.<P>86SE - I had a question about the compression thing, too, because I would like to build my 3.4 with a future turbo in mind. So, I bought a book on turbocharging. The book stated that compression on a turbo motor isn't critical like a blower motor and that 9:1 would work fine. The only thing it really says about building a motor for turbo applications is not to use a cam that has a lot of overlap.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000011||BBBAD GT||08-22-2000||10:40 AM||||9.0:1 is fine on a blower motor if you don't plan on boosting the snot out of it ( &gt;15lb of boost).  I'm running right at 14-15lb of Vortech S trim boost with my 5.0.  8.0:1 would be too low in my book if you plan on running around 10lb, because it would feel a little slugish off boost.  <P>I think a turbo/intercooled 3.4 would be perfect for the Fiero.  <P>  <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/BBBAD_GT_SIG.gif">   <P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT</A><BR><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000012||88 Fiero||08-22-2000||12:03 PM||||guys....Bob York's '88 gt has a 3.4 with a 9 compression..he got an watercooled turbo and the turbo charger camed from a 300ZX... he got the boost set at 7 lbs. and he got an auto in which has a different ratio in it...it can still leave a 6 foot skid mark on the pavement...he is ready to set the boost up to 10 lbs....this car is very wicked...||||reg||1
Z||000013||DjKid||08-22-2000||12:44 PM||||Whats the differance in the 94 Camaro V6 and the 95 V6.. the 95 is supposed to have like 200hp .. more than the 94?? from what my dad told me, his 95 had more power than his 94 did.<P>------------------<BR>DjKid - 88 Coupe<BR>'Blue Knight"||||reg||5
Z||000014||mrfiero||08-22-2000||12:56 PM||||The '93-'95 V-6 Camaros & Firebirds have identical 3.4 engines rated at 160 HP.  In '96 they switched to a 3.8 V-6 with 200 HP.....this is still the base engine today.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Alex4mula||08-22-2000||01:20 PM||||Went to two Dyno shops today. These are Stang guys. One didn't want the bussines. The other told me $80 an hour and $100 with A/F metering. Need to set appointment. Will check another one and if not better then will set that appointment. I'm very curious.. They only work 8-6 M-F. Will have to take work time for it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000016||86SE||08-22-2000||01:30 PM||||The 93-95 Camaro engines are supposed to have around 180 HP with the Fieros intake system.  Thats what I heard anyways.<P>With more intake and exhaust work im sure you can get it to 200bHP easy.  That would be about 165 rear wheel horse power.  2.8 puts out about 120 RWHP.<P>Plus the 3.4 has 30+ more lbs of torque.  cool<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero<BR>Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention||||reg||14
Z||000017||LarryB||08-22-2000||01:32 PM||||Didn't someone post some dyno results from their 3.4 on the forum earlier this year?  Something like 178HP and just over 200 ft-lbs peak?<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000018||GT Bastard||08-22-2000||01:40 PM||||86SE, check your email.||||reg||1
Z||000019||BBBAD GT||08-22-2000||05:42 PM||||Everything I've read about RWHP on stock V6's has been around the 100 mark.  Maybe 1FST2M6 can add to this, he pratically lives at a dyno  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <P>Have you tried the guys at Unlimited Performance in Delray Beach?  They are hard core 5.0 freaks, with a 1,000 rwhp stang they drive on the street, that does low 8's on command.  Joe is the guy to get ahold of there.  ||||reg||1
Z||000020||Alex4mula||08-23-2000||01:03 PM||||Unlimited Performance was the one that told me to go elswere because they have so much Stang business that cannot work with other cars. To me that sucks. It was not for free. Anyway with that attitude I wouldn't recommend them. And I have several other Fiero friends here interested in that service. Lucky they are not the only ones..||||reg||1
Z||000021||1FST2M6||08-23-2000||01:42 PM||||sorry i haven't been paying attention... uum $100 an hour is about right for TUNING! you just want a simple data log with A/F and 3 passes.. HP/TQ, HP/TQ, and all gear. should run you about $65 for the run WITH the exhaust gas analyzer in the pipe...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Unlimited Performance.. home of the worlds ONLY $100,000 brakes on a mustang! oh how sweet! but a million bucks for a INSANLY fast car it should have somthing cool!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Well if they are TOO swamped.. just make a few phone calls... just let them know you were referred to them by ANOTHER Dynojet equipped shop. Engineered Performance Inc. in atlanta... toss a name or two around and maybe that'll help... if not I'll call them! and set somthing up...  200hp is a difficult number... I was at 160 on my 2.8 with lots of bolt ons and with a little head work it will be easily grasp but if the 3.4 only makes 180 even with a cam swap don't be surprised if you only pull 190! but the torque should easily exceed 200lbft! ||||reg||1
Z||000022||86SE||08-23-2000||08:25 PM||||The reason I would start with a lower compression (8.5) is because as soon as you start adding boost, your compression will rise and it will get to the point you HAVE to run 92 octane for it to run properly.  And if the compression gets too high you will have to run 110 race fuel, I think that is about $2 a gallon.<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero<BR>Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention||||reg||1
Z||000023||Dan86gt3.8||08-24-2000||01:36 AM||||well regular gas is almost up to that price so why not just run the race fuel?  ||||reg||1
Z||000024||BBBAD GT||08-24-2000||09:25 AM||||I think its pretty much understood if you have anykind of forced induction, you run the most octane available (normally 93-94).  If you grip about that, then you don't need forced induction.  IMO, you can run pump gas (93-94 of course) on a street motor up to 15lb of boost, you just have to really set back the tune.  I'm proof of it (9:1 motor with 13-14lb of boost).  Now, if you want all the HP a forced induction motor can give, then you start upping the timing with race gas (and probably a little more fuel to be safe) and you'll realize at least 20-30 hp gain.  <BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000025||86SE||08-26-2000||02:49 AM||||If you put race fuel in a Fiero it would not run right at all.  The higher the octane, the slower the burning.  I'm pretty sure the Fiero engine recommends 87-89 Octane.<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero<BR>Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention||||reg||1
